  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The 1989 NCAA Men's Basketball Championship saw the Michigan Wolverines win their first national title by defeating the Seton Hall Pirates 80-79 in an overtime thriller on April 3, 1989, at the Kingdome in Seattle. Led by interim coach Steve Fisher and tournament MVP Glen Rice, Michigan secured the win on free throws by Rumeal Robinson. Key Details of the 1989 Championship: Champion: Michigan Wolverines (1st title). Runner-up: Seton Hall Pirates. Final Score: 80–79 (Overtime). Most Outstanding Player (MOP): Glen Rice (Michigan), who set a tournament record at the time with 184 points. Winning Coach: Steve Fisher, who took over just before the tournament after Bill Frieder was dismissed. Final Four Teams: Michigan, Seton Hall, Illinois, and Duke. Game Highlights & Context: The Finish: Seton Hall led 79-78 with three seconds left in overtime when Rumeal Robinson was fouled on a drive, dubbed \"The Phantom Foul\" by some fans, and hit both free throws to win. Record Setter: Glen Rice scored 31 points in the championship game and averaged 30.7 points over the tournament. Coaching Change: Michigan’s athletic director Bo Schembechler forced out head coach Bill Frieder before the tournament began when Frieder announced he would leave for Arizona State, appointing assistant coach Steve Fisher in his place.",
